<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext policy.

  The host itself makes no cleartext calls: there is not a single "http://" literal under
  app/src/main/java/com/kiyoscripts/nexusclone/. The permission exists only because cloned
  guest applications inherit this configuration (a guest runs inside the host process, so
  NetworkSecurityPolicy reads the host's config), and plenty of real apps still talk
  plaintext HTTP to their own backends.

  WHY THIS IS NOT SCOPED TO LOOPBACK:

  Scoping cleartext to localhost is the better posture for the host, and it was tried. It
  was reverted because it breaks the container's core function: a guest calling
  http://api.example.com gets CleartextNotPermittedException from OkHttp or
  HttpURLConnection, and running exactly those apps is the point of the product. Android's
  network security config has no per-uid or per-process scoping and no wildcard domain, so
  "cleartext for guests, never for the host" cannot be expressed here.

  The residual risk is therefore accepted and bounded: the host makes no cleartext calls of
  its own, so the only traffic this permits is guest traffic that would have been cleartext
  on the real device anyway. If the host ever gains an HTTP client, give that client its own
  HTTPS-only configuration rather than tightening this file.

  Do not widen this to a third-party host without recording why.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
